  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the material flows and infrastructure of a mine site, linking the underground ore body to surface waste handling and mineral processing. An ore deposit is shown beneath layers of overburden, with mining creating stepped excavations and generating two main outputs: crude ore and waste rock. The waste rock is placed in a large terraced dump, while fine leftover material from processing is stored separately as tailings behind dams in a tailings storage area.\n\nThe diagram also shows how crude ore moves through the site. It is hauled by road to crushing, then transported to concentration and chemical separation ponds, where valuable minerals are separated from the rest. The useful product leaves as concentrated metal, while tailings are directed back to the tailings impoundment.\n\nWater management appears to be an important part of the system. A water pipeline connects site infrastructure and the processing area, suggesting recirculation or supply of water needed for ore treatment. Energy and road access are included as supporting infrastructure. Overall, the image presents mining as an integrated system that extracts ore, processes it, and manages large volumes of waste rock, tailings, water, and energy.",
